# .citation-prior-tracker — every `#NNN` citation, in a tracked markdown
# file outside the dated-artefact directories, that already exceeded this
# repo's live tracker maximum on the date below (#261). A committed
# baseline, not a date-scoped predicate — see the "citation ceiling law"
# docblock in `packages/server/src/doc-citation-law.test.ts` for why, and
# for the rejected alternative.
#
# This is an EXPECTED-VIOLATION list, not a skip list: every entry is swept
# on every run, a new violation outside this file turns the law red, and an
# entry whose citation is no longer present in its file — or never exceeded
# the ceiling below in the first place — is itself a failure, so the list
# cannot silently drift out of date. Adding an entry that isn't wave-7's own
# #66 coupling (see the docblock) should be rare enough to warrant asking why
# a document just cited a number above the live maximum at all.
#
# The `live-maximum=` field below is LOAD-BEARING, not a note: it is the
# ceiling these rows were measured against, and it is what the staleness
# check compares them to. It is deliberately NOT the current ceiling. A row
# records that a citation exceeded the ceiling on the date it was taken, and
# that is a historical fact which does not expire when this repo files its
# next issue. Checking it against a ceiling that climbs made an unmodified
# tree go red — `#267 no longer exceeds the live maximum (268)` — and the
# remedy it prescribed was wrong: `docs/roadmap.md` cites `#267` meaning the
# prior tracker's issue, while live `#267` is an unrelated beacon collector,
# so deleting the row would have made the corpus permanently accept a
# citation that now resolves to something else. Re-measure the field
# deliberately, in a reviewed commit; never edit it to make a run go green.
#
# Format: <repo-relative file>  #<number>, one per (file, number) pair —
# duplicate citations of the same number in the same file collapse to one
# entry, since existence does not depend on repeat count.
#
